# Line endings, settled once.
#
# The repo was committed from a machine with core.autocrlf=false, so whatever
# was on disk went into the index verbatim: most sources were stored CRLF, a
# few LF, and one Cargo.toml was mixed inside itself. Anyone cloning with the
# Git-for-Windows default (autocrlf=true) would have had their next commit
# renormalise every CRLF file, producing whole-file diffs unrelated to their
# change — and `cargo fmt --all` did the same thing for the same reason.
#
# `text=auto` stores text as LF and checks it out however the platform wants.
* text=auto

# Except the two that Windows itself reads, which want CRLF on disk.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# And the shell scripts, which want LF wherever they are checked out. CI runs
# `scripts/verify-vendor.sh` on the windows leg as well as the macOS one, and
# bash reads a CRLF script as commands with a trailing carriage return —
# `$'': command not found`, on a line that looks perfectly fine in a diff.
*.sh text eol=lf

# And the one thing here that is not text.
*.ico binary

# The vendored dependency is not ours to renormalise. `scripts/verify-vendor.sh`
# compares it byte for byte against the .crate tarball from crates.io, and that
# tarball is LF — so `text=auto` above, which checks out CRLF wherever the
# platform asks for it, would fail that comparison on any clone made with the
# Git-for-Windows default, CI's own windows leg included. `-text` means never
# convert in either direction, which is also what keeps a `cargo fmt` or an
# editor from producing a 181-file diff through somebody else's code.
vendor/** -text
